# 4. Establish a Baseline Before Making Changes

Before implementation, identify what currently works and what already fails.

Run the relevant existing commands where possible:

- Dependency installation or integrity verification.
- Existing type checks.
- Existing lint checks.
- Existing unit tests.
- Existing integration tests.
- Existing production build.
- Existing E2E tests.

Do not invent commands. Discover the real scripts and tools first.

For each command, record:

- Exact command.
- Whether it was actually executed.
- Exit status.
- Relevant failure output.
- Environment limitations.
- Whether the failure existed before your changes.

Classify existing problems separately from regressions introduced by your implementation.

Do not silently treat pre-existing failures as successful checks.

A baseline failure must not become an excuse to weaken future quality gates. Where necessary, document the existing blocker and establish a narrowly scoped, visible exception rather than disabling an entire test suite.

