# 8. Test Runtime, Database, and Data Isolation

Testing must never endanger production data.

Before running integration or E2E tests, determine exactly where test data will be stored and which services the tests will access.

Requirements:

- Use isolated test databases or local test bindings.
- Never point tests at production databases.
- Never use production credentials for test execution.
- Use unique test records or fixtures.
- Ensure parallel tests do not interfere with each other.
- Clean up only records created by the relevant test.
- Never perform broad database wipes.
- Never modify or delete existing user data.
- Do not alter production schemas or migrations for this mission.

If the application uses D1, use an appropriate isolated local database configuration where supported by the actual architecture.

If safe isolation cannot be established, do not execute the potentially destructive test. Report it as `BLOCKED`.

External services such as market-data providers, currency services, weather services, or AI APIs must not be required for ordinary unit and integration test execution unless the specific test is explicitly designed for that purpose.

Use controlled test doubles at external boundaries. Keep core application logic real.

Separate external-service contract or integration checks from the ordinary reliable CI gates.

