# 15. Implementation Strategy: Small, Reversible Stages

Do not make all changes at once.

Implement the work in logical stages and validate each stage before proceeding.

## Stage 1 — Repository protection and baseline

- Inspect the repository.
- Protect existing work.
- Discover the architecture.
- Identify existing tools.
- Run baseline checks.
- Separate existing failures from new failures.

## Stage 2 — Dependency and execution reproducibility

- Preserve the existing package manager.
- Correct missing or inconsistent lockfile arrangements when necessary.
- Align the runtime version with repository conventions.
- Verify deterministic installation.
- Avoid unnecessary package upgrades.

## Stage 3 — Quality commands and core testing

- Configure reliable type checking.
- Configure linting.
- Preserve existing test runners.
- Establish meaningful unit and integration test commands.
- Add only justified tests.

## Stage 4 — Application and runtime integration

- Add relevant API contract coverage.
- Add required negative and boundary tests.
- Establish isolated test data and database execution.
- Validate important application execution paths.

## Stage 5 — Browser and E2E validation

- Configure the appropriate browser testing runtime.
- Test real critical user workflows.
- Add limited desktop and mobile smoke checks.
- Add proportionate accessibility checks.

## Stage 6 — CI implementation

- Integrate the required quality checks.
- Apply workflow security restrictions.
- Configure appropriate caching and timeouts.
- Separate CI from deployment.
- Preserve existing CI conventions.

## Stage 7 — Final regression and independent verification

- Run the relevant checks again.
- Verify the production build.
- Inspect all changes.
- Review test quality.
- Perform targeted mutation testing where feasible.
- Confirm no unintended product, data, architecture, or UI changes occurred.

After each stage, inspect the diff and validate its results.

If a stage introduces a regression, correct it before building further changes on top of the broken state.

Do not use destructive rollback operations. Revert only changes that you can positively identify as your own, and preserve all pre-existing user work.

