# 16. Acceptance Criteria

The mission is complete only when the actual implementation has been validated against the applicable requirements below.

## A. Repository integrity

- Existing user modifications have been preserved.
- No unrelated files have been overwritten or deleted.
- No destructive Git operation has been used.
- No automatic commit, push, deployment, or release has occurred.

## B. Dependency reproducibility

- The package manager has been identified correctly.
- Dependency installation follows the selected lockfile.
- No unnecessary package-manager migration has occurred.
- No broad dependency upgrade has occurred.

## C. Quality commands

- The relevant type-check command works.
- The relevant lint command works.
- Test commands execute actual tests.
- Production build executes independently of development mode.
- Commands return meaningful exit statuses.

## D. Test quality

- Important existing functionality has meaningful regression protection.
- Critical APIs have appropriate contract coverage where applicable.
- Negative and boundary cases are covered where relevant.
- Numeric calculations are tested against defensible expectations.
- Integration tests use real integration boundaries where feasible.
- E2E tests verify actual behavior rather than element existence alone.
- Tests do not depend unnecessarily on production services or data.
- Test failures are not hidden through weakened assertions or disabled tests.

## E. Runtime and data safety

- Tests use isolated data and runtime configuration.
- Production services and data are not modified.
- Existing migrations and schemas remain unchanged.
- Runtime limitations are accurately documented.

## F. CI

- The CI workflow uses the appropriate runtime and package manager.
- CI permissions are restricted.
- Secrets are protected.
- CI is separate from deployment.
- The required checks are connected to the appropriate workflow.
- Unexecuted remote workflows are not reported as successful.

## G. Scope and regression protection

- Product business logic remains unchanged.
- Pricing logic and market datasets remain unchanged.
- User-facing design and behavior remain unchanged.
- Authentication behavior remains unchanged.
- No unrelated architecture refactoring has occurred.

## H. Evidence

- Every claimed successful check has actual execution evidence.
- Existing failures are distinguished from newly introduced failures.
- Blocked, skipped, and unimplemented checks are clearly identified.
- Important changes can be reviewed through Git diffs.

Do not invent coverage targets such as 80% merely to satisfy an arbitrary metric. Establish meaningful coverage of critical behavior first. Report actual coverage if the existing tools support it.

A green test suite alone is not sufficient if its assertions are weak, its tests are skipped, or its runtime does not represent the real application.

